$DIR/main.p:6:6: a function literal that captures variables cannot be started with go: out lives in the starting frame, which may be gone before the puroutine runs (spec section 4.7); pass them as arguments instead: go func(out chan int, i int) {...}(out, i)
    A function literal that uses variables of the function around it reaches
    them in that function's stack frame. A go statement may start it after that
    function has returned, when the frame is gone.
    Start the literal with a sync.Scope instead: s.Task(func() {...}) waits for
    it before s's block ends, so it may use the function's variables, a Mutex or
    a WaitGroup among them. Or pass the values the literal needs as arguments:
    they are copied when the go statement runs. Do not pass a Mutex, a WaitGroup
    or another sync type this way, since the copy is a different one.
    Go moves captured variables to the heap. Plutonium never allocates behind
    the program's back, so captured variables stay in their frame (spec section
    11.5).
    Pass the values as arguments:
        for i := 0; i < n; i++ {
            go func(i int, out chan int) {
                out <- i * i
            }(i, out)
        }
    (see "Task statements" in the Plutonium spec, section 12.12)
    (run "pluto explain E2590" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:10:2: go with the built-in println is not supported; start a function that calls it
    A go statement cannot start a built-in function such as println or close
    directly. Wrap it in a function and start that.
    (see "Task statements" in the Plutonium spec, section 12.12)
    (run "pluto explain E2591" for a longer explanation)
